ince I don't have the complete faithfulness breakdown—I can work with the mean ablation metrics showing CSS at 1.0 and CPR at 0.112, though I'll need to present this more concisely than the fully detailed tables I have for SVA and Qwen. The paper should still cover both IOI and Arithmetic for GPT-2 as core data points, so I'm figuring out how to balance comprehensive coverage with the data I actually have available.\n\nFor IOI GPT-2, the mean ablation gives me CSS=1.0 with 18 edges and CPR=0.112, plus a best single CPR of 0.091, but I'm missing the per-method breakdown. For Arithmetic GPT-2, I have CSS=1.0 with 54 edges and CPR=0.919, and I'm working through the css_advantage metric to determine the best single CPR—if the advantage is -0.066, that would put best single at 0.985, though that seems quite high and I should verify this calculation against what I know from the SVA results.\n\nLooking at the Qwen Arithmetic data, EAP-IG shows CPR=0.971, and for GPT-2 Arithmetic I have max_jaccard=0.695 with css_advantage=-0.066, which would give best_single=0.985 if CSS CPR is 0.919—plausible for a highly distributed task where methods overlap significantly. I have enough to write this up now, sticking to the JSON numbers and presenting summary metrics where I don't have full method-by-method breakdowns. I also noticed the SVA data shows css_1.0_isinf at 22, which indicates some degeneration cases to account for.\n\nThe isinf degeneration is critical for Claim 3—CSS under isinf equals ACDC isinf exactly, confirming the degeneration pattern. Now I'm structuring the paper with an abstract, introduction, background section, then the method section covering circuit discovery, CSS definition, top-k normalization, and CSS as a diagnostic tool, followed by experimental setup and results sections.\n\nI need to organize the results across multiple subsections covering faithfulness metrics, Jaccard-utility correlations, binarization pathology analysis, CSS versus CIRCUS comparisons, and sensitivity studies. Then I'll wrap up with discussion, limitations, and conclusion sections. The paper will incorporate all completed experimental data including GPT-2 SVA faithfulness with Jaccard scores, Qwen IOI faithfulness with Jaccard, Qwen arithmetic faithfulness, and binarization sensitivity tables.\n\nI'm consolidating the results into a comprehensive Table 1 that spans all five model-task combinations, capturing CSS performance at full edge retention, CPR metrics for both CSS and the best single method, the advantage CSS provides, and maximum Jaccard scores. This single table will serve as the core empirical contribution, with additional tables breaking down ablation comparisons between methods on IOI, CSS versus CIRCUS performance, and Jaccard-CSS correlations. Looking at the performance metrics across these different model and method combinations, I'm verifying the advantage calculations—for GPT-2 on Arithmetic, the CSS score of 0.919 minus the advantage of -0.066 gives the best single method performance of 0.985, which checks out. The same logic applies to Qwen's Arithmetic results where the advantage of -0.039 correctly reflects the gap between the combined approach and the best individual method.\n\nNow examining the SVA results more closely, I see that CSS achieves strong compression with just 23 edges while maintaining a CPR of 0.941, compared to EAP-IG's 75 edges at 0.953—so the advantage of -0.012 reflects how close they are. What's striking about the IOI task on GPT-2 is that despite CSS using far fewer edges (18 versus 75), it achieves a notably higher CPR of 0.112 versus 0.091, which represents a meaningful performance gain.\n\nFor Qwen IOI, I'm verifying the numbers: CSS reaches 0.103 while the best single method hits 0.091, giving CSS a +0.012 advantage that checks out. Now I'm ready to structure the paper with a proper LaTeX setup.\n\nSetting up the document...\n\nNow I'm outlining the paper structure with key framing decisions: the title should highlight the analytical framework, the abstract needs to be transparent about the small sample size and observed patterns, and the introduction should establish why circuit discovery methods produce conflicting results. I'll organize the method section around formally defining consensus scoring, structure results by individual claims, and use the discussion to examine the relationship between IFR and EP while acknowledging the limitations of working with a small model.\n\nFor the paper itself, I'm deciding between a few title options that emphasize the diagnostic nature of consensus scoring, and I'll write it with inline references rather than managing a separate bibliography file. I need to be strategic about what goes in the main paper versus the appendix—keeping the consolidated results, correlation tables, and method comparisons in the main text while relegating per-task details and ablation studies to supplementary material.\n\nNow I'm working through the specific numbers for the consolidated results table. For GPT-2 IOI, CSS achieves perfect agreement at 1.0 with just 18 edges and a CPR of 0.112, compared to the best single method's 75 edges and 0.091 CPR, giving CSS an advantage of 0.021. For GPT-2 SVA, CSS again hits 1.0 but with 23 edges and a CPR of 0.941, while the best single method uses 75 edges with 0.953 CPR—here CSS actually has a slight disadvantage of -0.012. I'm still pulling together the individual method CPRs for GPT-2 Arithmetic to complete this picture.\n\nWait, I'm noticing a discrepancy in the IOI numbers—the binarization sweep shows 18 edges at k=75, but the CIRCUS comparison table lists only 7 edges for CSS=1.0. These must be from different experimental setups or different k values, so I need to figure out which data is relevant for the consolidated table.\n\nLooking more carefully at the CIRCUS comparison, the 7 edges might represent full consensus across all methods without top-k filtering, whereas the 18 edges come from the